WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.283
Multi-tenancy and access control.

2
00:00:02.563 --> 00:00:10.328
If your system serves more than one customer, team, or permission level, the most damaging retrieval failure is not a wrong answer.

3
00:00:10.608 --> 00:00:14.576
It is a right answer from a document the user was not allowed to see.

4
00:00:14.856 --> 00:00:30.152
OWASP’s 2025 entry on vector and embedding weaknesses lists exactly this: weak access controls exposing sensitive embedded data, and cross-context leaks in multi-tenant systems that share one vector database.

5
00:00:30.432 --> 00:00:39.499
It also notes that embeddings can be partially inverted to recover source text, so treat vectors as being as sensitive as the documents they came from.

6
00:00:39.779 --> 00:00:44.963
The isolation mechanisms differ by store: Namespaces or collections per tenant.

7
00:00:45.243 --> 00:00:54.309
Pinecone recommends one namespace per tenant: each is stored separately, which gives physical isolation and makes offboarding a tenant one delete.

8
00:00:54.589 --> 00:01:02.952
Qdrant, by contrast, says a collection per tenant is rarely efficient and is best kept for a few tenants that need strict isolation.

9
00:01:03.232 --> 00:01:05.109
One collection with a tenant field.

10
00:01:05.389 --> 00:01:15.949
Qdrant’s recommended default for many small tenants: a group_id -style payload field filtered on every request, with the payload index marked is_tenant (version 1.

11
00:01:16.229 --> 00:01:19.579
11 and later) so each tenant’s data is stored together.

12
00:01:19.859 --> 00:01:32.339
Its docs add a subtle warning: BM25 and IDF statistics are shared across tenants by default, so keyword scores can be influenced by other tenants’ documents unless you scope them.

13
00:01:32.619 --> 00:01:33.984
Row-level security.

14
00:01:34.264 --> 00:01:42.179
In Postgres, a row security policy makes the database itself add the tenant condition to every query for a given role.

15
00:01:42.459 --> 00:01:57.733
Mind the Postgres docs’ caveats: superusers and roles with the BYPASSRLS attribute always bypass row security, and table owners do too unless you set FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY, so the application must connect as an ordinary role.

16
00:01:58.013 --> 00:02:05.139
A table with row security enabled and no policy denies everything, which is the fail-closed default you want.

17
00:02:05.419 --> 00:02:08.043
Whatever the mechanism, the rules are the same.

18
00:02:08.323 --> 00:02:15.320
The tenant and group values come from the authenticated session on the server, never from request input or from the model.

19
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:23.195
The query path makes the filter impossible to skip (a required argument, a policy in the database, a per-tenant client).

20
00:02:23.475 --> 00:02:28.808
Permission changes in the source system propagate to the index on a schedule you can state.

21
00:02:29.088 --> 00:02:35.979
And a test plants canary documents in one tenant and fails the build if any query from another tenant ever returns them.

22
00:02:36.259 --> 00:02:41.784
Log every retrieval with the identity and filter used, so you can answer “who could have seen this?

23
00:02:42.064 --> 00:02:43.301
” after an incident.
